# 📊 What a SOC Analyst Looks For

When analyzing authentication logs, pay attention to:

| Field                 | Why it matters                       |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Timestamp             | When the event happened              |
| Username              | Which account was targeted           |
| Source IP             | Where the connection came from       |
| Authentication result | Success or failure                   |
| Service               | SSH or another service               |
| Frequency             | Is activity repeated?                |
| Sequence              | Failed attempts followed by success? |

---

# 🔐 Cybersecurity Relevance

Linux logs are one of the most important sources of evidence during security investigations.

They can help detect:

* Brute-force attempts
* Unauthorized logins
* Suspicious SSH activity
* Account compromise
* Privilege escalation indicators
* Service failures
* Persistence activity

Basic SOC workflow:

```text
Log Event
    ↓
Identify Timestamp
    ↓
Identify User
    ↓
Identify Source IP
    ↓
Check Success/Failure
    ↓
Look for Repeated Activity
    ↓
Correlate With Other Logs
    ↓
Investigate the Process/Activity
```

